FAILURES:

- ACCESSIBILITY.md point 8 (status conveyed by color must not mislead;
  read together with the recorded ruling 2026-08-29a: "a threshold applied
  to a figure must live in ONE classification source consumed by every
  surface that renders it") — web/templates/components/kpis.html:129-145.
  The Budget KPI card's own CONTAINER TINT is computed from a bare-sign
  comparison with no dead-band:
      {{$budgetOver := and .Metrics.HasCombinedTarget (gt .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta 0.0)}}
      {{$budgetHealthy := and .Metrics.HasCombinedTarget (le .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta 0.0)}}
  while the SAME card's headline three lines below re-implements the $1
  dead-band with its own hardcoded literals instead of reading
  `.BudgetVerdict.IsOver`/`.IsUnder`/`.Health`:
      {{if gt .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta 1.0}} ... over
      {{else if lt .Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta -1.0}} ... under
      {{else}} On budget {{end}}
  Independently reproduced (own fixture, not copied from checker-second):
  CombinedCumulativeDelta=0.60, HasCombinedTarget=true, both buckets
  configured (BuildBudgetVerdict classifies this as Health=green,
  IsOver=false, IsUnder=false — i.e. neutral/fine). Rendered `kpis`
  output:
      <div class="... border-rose-200 dark:border-rose-800 bg-rose-50 dark:bg-rose-900/20" ...>
        <p ...>Budget</p>
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">On budget</p>
        ...Living: $2,000.60 of $2,000.00 ... <span class="num">$0.60</span> on target
        ...Health: $300.00 of $300.00 ... <span class="num">$0.00</span> on target
  A danger-colored (rose/red) card chrome wraps a headline reading
  "On budget" and two rows both reading "on target" — for the identical
  figure (CombinedCumulativeDelta == BudgetVerdict.Delta) two independent,
  disagreeing classifiers render on the same card. This is not "color
  conveys meaning alone" in the narrowest textual sense — text is present
  — but the color and text channels actively contradict each other on the
  same data point, which defeats the purpose of point 8's own
  color+text pairing requirement: a sighted user scanning the tint as the
  fast signal (exactly what point 8's pairing is meant to support) is told
  "over/danger," while the text says "fine." Reachable whenever
  0 < CombinedCumulativeDelta <= $1.00 (rose tint / neutral text) or
  -$1.00 <= CombinedCumulativeDelta < 0 (emerald tint / neutral text, a
  milder but same-shaped mismatch). Confirms checker-second's
  independently-filed W4.3 FAIL (same file/lines, same root cause); I
  reproduced it myself from scratch rather than taking that verdict on
  faith, and get the same rendered contradiction. Not caught by the boss's
  four-item recheck list because none of items (1)-(4) inspect the card's
  own outer border/bg classes against its own headline/rows — but it is
  squarely inside ACCESSIBILITY.md's scope for this diff's files, and it
  is the same defect FAMILY (bare-sign vs. dead-banded classification of
  one figure, two surfaces) this very task exists to eliminate, just
  un-fixed on the card's tint rather than its rows.

  Fix should have the card's tint consume `.BudgetVerdict.Health` (or
  `.IsOver`/`.IsUnder`) instead of re-deriving `$budgetOver`/
  `$budgetHealthy` from `.Metrics.CombinedCumulativeDelta` directly, the
  same way this attempt already made the per-bucket rows consume
  `.BudgetVerdict.{Living,Healthcare}` instead of re-deriving from
  `.Metrics.{Cumulative,Healthcare}Delta`.

